/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Self inductance L is the flux linkage per ampere in one coil.
   - L is self inductance in henries (H); N is the number of turns (no unit); R is the reluctance of the magnetic path in ampere-turns per weber (A·t/Wb) or H⁻¹.
   - g is air-gap length in metres; μ₀ is free-space permeability = 4π × 10⁻⁷ H/m; A is the gap area in square metres.
   - An iron core with a coil and a movable armature has a narrow air gap g.
   - A second coil can be placed on the same magnetic path.
   - M is mutual inductance in henries (H); L₁ and L₂ are the self inductances of the two coils in henries; k is the coupling coefficient, 0 ≤ k ≤ 1.
   - In the mutual-inductance transducer, coil 1 is the primary and coil 2 is the secondary.
   - For a simple air gap, L ∝ 1/g.
   - S is sensitivity in henries per metre (H/m); the negative sign shows L falls when g increases; other symbols are as above.
   - The inductance exists with a steady current too, but a steady current gives no voltage across it (v = L·di/dt), so the read-out needs AC excitation, typically a few kilohertz.
*/
