/* book exam traps: Treating L as linear in gap. L ∝ 1/g, so a 20% gap rise gives about 16.7% fall, not 20%. | Using a DC ohmmeter to measure inductance. A coil's resistance is not its inductance; AC excitation and a bridge or meter are needed. | Forgetting the air gap dominates. Iron reluctance is small, but at very small gaps it can matter; do not use L = μ₀AN²/g blindly for a closed core. | Mixing self and mutual inductance. L uses one coil; M uses two coils and k. M = k√(L₁L₂), not L₁ + L₂. | Ignoring sign and direction. L falls as gap rises; a larger gap gives lower output. Phase can indicate direction only in a suitable circuit. */
