/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An LVDT is a transformer built on a hollow insulating tube called the former.
   - The two secondaries are connected in series opposition, so the terminals give the difference of their voltages.
   - V₁ and V₂ are the rms voltages induced in S₁ and S₂, in volts; x is the core displacement from the centre position, in millimetres, positive toward S₁; K is the sensitivity in volts per millimetre at the excitation actually used.
   - The primary is excited from an AC source, usually a few volts rms at a frequency between about 1 kHz and 10 kHz.
   - Magnitude alone cannot separate the two sides of null, because a plain voltmeter reads the same on both.
   - Displacement is then read from x = Vout / K.
   - The core floats, so friction and hysteresis are very low and the life is long.
*/
