/* book Worked example: Q: An LVDT is excited at 5 V rms. At a core position the secondary voltages are V₁ = 2.00 V rms and V₂ = 1.60 V rms. The sensitivity is 0.50 V/mm at that 5 V rms excitation. Find (a) the differential output, (b) the displacement, (c) the direction, (d) the output at null.
   1. Differential output: Vout = V₁ − V₂ = 2.00 − 1.60 = 0.40 V rms.
   2. The constant K = 0.50 V/mm applies at the 5 V rms drive used here; the per-volt figure is S = 100 mV/(mm·V), and 100 mV/(mm·V) × 5 V = 0.50 V/mm.
   3. x = Vout / K = 0.40 / 0.50 = 0.80 mm.
   4. V₁ is larger than V₂, so the core has moved toward S₁, the side whose secondary gained voltage. Direction: 0.80 mm toward S₁.
   5. At null the core is centred, so V₁ = V₂ and Vout = 0 V rms ideally; a real unit leaves a few millivolts of residual voltage.
   6. The same 0.40 V rms reading would appear at 0.80 mm on the S₂ side, but with opposite phase. Only the phase separates the two.
   Answer: Vout = 0.40 V rms, displacement = 0.80 mm toward S₁, and the ideal null output is 0 V. */
